---
/**
 * One published figure on /ops, with the thing that makes it a measurement
 * rather than a claim: where it came from.
 *
 * EVERY NUMBER STATES ITS SOURCE (06 §1). A page that publishes figures without
 * saying which system produced them is asking to be trusted rather than
 * checked, which is the opposite of what /ops is for -- so `source` is required
 * and there is no arm of this component that renders a value without one.
 *
 * ABSENT IS A STATE, NOT A ZERO. `value === null` means this build could not
 * read the figure, and it renders a quiet labelled absence in the slot the
 * figure would have occupied: the same size band, the muted ink, no error
 * colour and no empty box. A zero would be a lie in the one place nobody would
 * think to audit -- "0 agents served" reads as a measurement, and a reader has
 * no way to tell it from a query that never ran. The two causes this page
 * actually has are told apart by `absentState`/`absentReason` rather than
 * flattened into one sentence, because "the credential is missing" and "the
 * store did not answer" call for different actions from whoever reads it.
 */
interface Props {
  /** What the figure counts. Sentence case; the component does the styling. */
  label: string;
  /**
   * The figure, ready to render. `null` means this build cannot say -- see the
   * absence props below.
   *
   * A STRING OR A NUMBER, never a `Date` or an object: this value is rendered
   * straight into the page, and the reader of /ops is owed a formatting
   * decision made by the caller that knows what the number means rather than by
   * `toString()`.
   */
  value: string | number | null;
  /**
   * Which system produced it. The closed set is the three systems /ops READS,
   * one per call in its frontmatter; a fourth means a new read, not a new
   * string.
   *
   * `'this build'` WAS A FOURTH ARM AND IS GONE (final-review Minor 8). No
   * `OpsMetric` ever passed it: the figures sourced that way -- the rate
   * limits, the retention windows, the changelog -- are lists rather than
   * single figures, so they are not this component at all and carry their own
   * hand-written provenance line. A stated closed set with a member nothing can
   * reach is a small false claim about the page, which is the class of defect
   * /ops exists to avoid.
   */
  source: 'D1' | 'Analytics Engine' | 'AI Gateway';
  /** The absence, in the two words that go where the figure was. */
  absentState?: string;
  /** Why it is absent, rendered after the source so the two read as one line. */
  absentReason?: string;
  /**
   * What this figure does NOT include, when that is not obvious from the label.
   * One sentence. Used by the requests figure, whose partiality is structural
   * (see wrangler.jsonc's `run_worker_first`) and whose label is the fix for it.
   */
  note?: string;
}

const {
  label,
  value,
  source,
  absentState = 'not configured',
  absentReason = 'this needs the read-only analytics token',
  note,
} = Astro.props;
---

{/*
    A FIGURE CARRYING A SENTENCE USED TO TAKE TWO COLUMNS, and the 2026-09
    redesign is why it no longer does. The old grid was `gap-10` over the page
    background, where a wide cell cost nothing. The band is a `hairline-grid`
    now: cells are tracks over a rule-coloured ground, so a tile spanning two
    of them leaves the last row short by one, and a short row in a hairline
    grid paints as a block of bare rule colour rather than as nothing. /ops
    carries ten figures and the band runs five across for the same arithmetic.
    The note reads as a quieter line inside its own cell instead, which is what
    `text-micro` is doing below.
  */}
{/*
    `data-ops-metric` is a TEST HOOK and says so. tests/ops-page.test.ts has to
    assert a property of ONE tile -- that the analytics figures render their
    absence and never a rendered zero -- and slicing the page on class names
    would make that assertion fail the next time a margin changes. Same
    convention as `data-testid="resume"` and `data-gap-notice` elsewhere in this
    repo. The label is the value because it is what the test looks a tile up by.
  */}
<div data-ops-metric={label} class="bg-surface px-6 pb-7 pt-[26px]">
  {/*
      The figure, or the absence in its place. `data-numeric` is what
      global.css turns into tabular figures -- so a column of these lines up
      rather than shimmering, which is the whole reason that rule names /ops.

      `data-metric-value` IS ON BOTH ARMS AND THAT IS THE POINT.
      tests/ops-page.test.ts reads every one of them out of the band and fails
      on an empty string, so the attribute has to sit on the absence as well:
      hooked to the figure alone it would assert nothing about the tiles that
      have no figure, which are the only ones that could ever have rendered a
      bare zero in the first place.
    */}
  {value === null ? (
    <p class="font-mono text-heading text-ink-muted" data-metric-value>
      {absentState}
    </p>
  ) : (
    <p class="font-display text-figure text-ink" data-metric-value data-numeric>
      {value}
    </p>
  )}
  <p class="mt-3 text-small text-ink-muted">{label}</p>
  {/*
      Provenance, quieter than the label on purpose: figure, then what it
      counts, then where it came from.

      THE TWO LINES TRADED CASES IN THE 2026-09 REDESIGN, and the note that used
      to be here said the opposite: it read "lower case and unspaced against the
      label's tracked capitals", which was true while the label was
      `font-mono text-micro uppercase`. The design inverts them. The label is
      sentence-case sans now and the source is the tracked mono capitals, so the
      third line is still told from the second by case and family rather than by
      a fourth type size the scale does not have. Only the direction changed.

      THE CAPITALS COME OFF WHEN THE FIGURE IS ABSENT. `absentReason` is a
      sentence, not a system name, and "ANALYTICS ENGINE — THIS NEEDS THE
      READ-ONLY ANALYTICS TOKEN" shouts an apology across the band. Uppercasing
      is CSS either way, so the text a test reads is the same in both arms.
    */}
  <p
    class:list={[
      'mt-1 font-mono text-micro text-ink-muted',
      value === null ? 'normal-case' : 'uppercase',
    ]}
  >
    {source}
    {value === null ? ` — ${absentReason}` : ''}
  </p>
  {note && <p class="mt-4 text-micro text-ink-muted">{note}</p>}
</div>
